// RS VR scopes, REAL mode (zscript/rsq/scope_real.zs).
//
// The surface the view down the bore is rendered into. One texture serves every scoped gun,
// because only one gun is ever the ready weapon: scope_real.zs re-binds it to
// the held scoped gun's lens and swaps it onto that gun's glass surface, and the engine only
// re-renders a camera texture in a frame where something drew it (hw_entrypoint.cpp). 2.0: on
// for as long as a scoped gun is held; with no scoped gun held it costs nothing at all.
//
// The name is eight characters on purpose: ParseCameraTexture looks it up with
// TEXMAN_ShortNameOnly, so a longer one would be registered and then never found again.
// It must match RSQ_ScopeReal.TEXNAME / TEXNAME_N.
//
// 256x256 square. Square because the view through a scope is a circle; 256 because this
// ships on a standalone Quest and the lens is a couple of hundred pixels across at most --
// the cost that matters is the extra scene render, not the resolution, but there is no point
// paying for pixels nobody can resolve.
// 2.0 scope-3x: "fit 240 200". The engine renders a camera texture at
// ratio = aspectRatio / pixelstretch (hw_entrypoint.cpp:400), and aspectRatio is fitwidth/fitheight
// (animations.cpp:791). Square (1.0) gave ratio 0.833: the picture came out squashed to 0.8 of its
// height on the square glass (rig: target 45 px wide but 43 px tall in the lens, 15 x 18 outside).
// 240/200 = 1.2 = MAP pixelstretch makes the ratio 1, so the 0..1 UV disc shows the world at the same
// scale across and up. The texture is still 256x256 pixels; fit only sets the ratio and display size.
cameratexture RSQSCOPE 256 256 fit 240 200
